L12@P0: Summary
L13@P0: With 500 000 cases of multidrug-resistant tuberculosis there is an urgent need for attractive targets to
L14@P0: enable the discovery of novel antimycobacterials. The
L15@P0: biosynthesis of essential cofactors is of particular
L16@P0: interest as these pathways are absent in man and
L17@P0: their inhibition is expected to affect the metabolism of
L18@P0: Mycobacterium tuberculosis at multiple sites. Our
L19@P0: data demonstrate that the pathogen synthesizes pyridoxal 5-phosphate (PLP), the bioactive form of
L20@P0: vitamin B6, by a heteromeric PLP synthase composed
L21@P0: of Pdx1 (Rv2606c) and Pdx2 (Rv2604c). Disruption of
L22@P0: the pdx1 gene generated a strictly B6 auxotrophic M.
L23@P0: tuberculosis mutant, Dpdx1. Removal of the cofactor
L24@P0: during exponential growth or stationary phase demonstrated the essentiality of vitamin B6 biosynthesis
L25@P0: for growth and survival of the pathogen in culture. In
L26@P0: a tuberculosis dormancy model based on gradual
L27@P0: oxygen depletion, de novo biosynthesis of PLP was
L28@P0: required for regrowth of the bacillus after direct
L29@P0: oxygen exposure. The Dpdx1 mutant showed a severe
L30@P0: growth defect in immunocompetent mice: bacilli
L31@P0: applied intranasally failed to persist in host tissues
L32@P0: and were quickly cleared. We conclude that vitamin
L33@P0: B6 biosynthesis is required for survival of M. tuberculosis in vivo and thus might represent a candidate
L34@P0: pathway for the development of new antitubercular
L35@P0: agents.
L36@P0: Introduction
L37@P0: Mycobacterium tuberculosis claims almost two million
L38@P0: lives annually and emerging drug resistance made the
L39@P0: situation more critical during the past decades (Espinal,
L40@P0: 2003; Harries and Dye, 2006). To combat tuberculosis
L41@P0: attractive drug targets are required to discover new and
L42@P0: effective antibiotics. Pathways present in the pathogen
L43@P0: but absent in the host are of particular interest. A whole
L44@P0: genome analysis predicted the capability of M. tuberculosis to synthesize vitamin B6, one of the essential components of the human diet (Cole et al., 1998). The term
L45@P0: ‘vitamin B6’ refers to the vitamers pyridoxine, pyridoxal,
L46@P0: pyridoxamine and their respective 5-phosphoesters. Two
L47@P0: independent pathways to generate pyridoxal 5-phosphate
L48@P0: (PLP), the bioactive form of vitamin B6 are known. They
L49@P0: were distinguished by involvement of the metabolite
L50@P0: deoxyxylose 5-phosphate (DXP) and named accordingly
L51@P0: (Fitzpatrick et al., 2007). The ‘DXP-dependent’ pathway
L52@P0: limited to a small number of bacteria consists of seven
L53@P0: enzymatic steps and was extensively studied in Escherichia coli (Lam and Winkler, 1990; 1992; Zhao and
L54@P0: Winkler, 1994; 1995; 1996; Zhao et al., 1995; Hill et al.,
L55@P0: 1996). The increasing availability of genomic data made
L56@P0: clear that the ‘DXP-independent’ route is wide-spread
L57@P0: among archaea, plants, fungi and most bacteria including
L58@P0: the tubercle bacillus (Mittenhuber, 2001). Remarkably,
L59@P0: about 4% of all classified enzymes depend on PLP as
L60@P0: cofactor, highlighting the enormous functional diversity of
L61@P0: the vitamin (Mozzarelli and Bettati, 2006; Fitzpatrick et al.,
L62@P0: 2007). M. tuberculosis possesses 58 genes encoding proteins that carry a PLP binding motif (Cole et al., 1998;
L63@P0: Percudani and Peracchi, 2009). Out of those genes 21
L64@P0: were identified as essential for growth in vitro and most of
L65@P0: them are functionally related to the amino acid metabolism (Sassetti et al., 2003). Further implications of PLP in
L66@P0: mycobacteria include the biosynthesis of vitamins B7
L67@P0: (biotin), B9 (folate) and B12 (cobalamin), the formation of
L68@P0: cell wall components and building blocks of DNA (Cole
L69@P0: et al., 1998; Strych et al., 2001; Percudani and Peracchi,
L70@P0: 2009). In addition, vitamin B6 was reported to be a potent
L71@P0: quencher of reactive oxygen intermediates (Ehrenshaft
L72@P0: et al., 1999; Jain and Lim, 2001; Matxain et al., 2009). The
L73@P0: ‘DXP-independent’ pathway consists of a bifunctional
L74@P0: enzyme complex, a class I glutamine amidotransferase
