L141@P2: A pdx1 loss-of-function mutant of M. tuberculosis is fully
L142@P2: auxotrophic for vitamin B6
L143@P2: To address the function of PLP biosynthesis in M. tuberculosis,a pdx1 knockout mutant (Dpdx1) was generated
L144@P2: via homologous recombination and confirmed by Southern blotting (Fig. S1). As a control, the knockout strain
L145@P2: was genetically complemented with a wild-type copy of
L146@P2: pdx1 (Dpdx1comp) under the control of its native promoter. The Dpdx1 mutant was not able to multiply in
L147@P2: vitamin B6-deficient medium, whereas wild-type M.
L148@P2: tuberculosis and the complemented strain showed
L149@P2: typical growth curves (Fig. 2A). This demonstrates that
L150@P2: PLP is essential for growth in vitro. We then analysed the
L151@P2: growth of Dpdx1 in the presence of varying concentrations of exogenous pyridoxine, a stable B6 vitamer, to
L152@P2: determine the pathogen’s uptake capability and physiological requirement for the vitamin. The chemical
L153@P2: complementation of the Dpdx1 strain with pyridoxine
L154@P2: was concentration dependent and optimal growth was
L155@P2: restored at 5 mM (Fig. 2A). Lower concentrations of pyridoxine up to 0.5 mM impaired replication, whereas 50 mM
L156@P2: proved to be slightly toxic.
