L185@P3: PLP biosynthesis of hypoxic non-replicating M.
L186@P3: tuberculosis is essential for direct regrowth on agar
L187@P3: Current working models suggest that the prolonged chemotherapy required to cure tuberculosis is due to a
L188@P3: defined subpopulation of hypoxic non-replicating bacilli.
L189@P3: The obligate aerobic pathogen requires oxygen for growth
L190@P3: but not for survival (Barry et al., 2009). Larry Wayne first
L191@P3: introduced a culture model based on gradual oxygen
L192@P3: deprivation to study non-replicating persistence of the
L193@P3: tubercle bacillus (Wayne and Hayes, 1996; Wayne and
L194@P3: Sohaskey, 2001). A Western blot analysis revealed that
L195@P3: wild-type M. tuberculosis upregulated the PLP synthase in
L196@P3: the Wayne model of dormancy (Fig. 3A). Compared with
L197@P3: the wild-type or the complemented strain, the Dpdx1
L198@P3: mutant showed a significant loss of viability in nonreplicating Wayne cultures (Fig. 3B, triangles, broken line)
L199@P3: when samples were directly plated on agar. However,
L200@P3: when the respective Dpdx1 samples were re-aerated in
L201@P3: liquid medium prior to plating, the colony-forming units
L202@P3: (cfu) were comparable to the controls (Fig. 3B, triangles,
L203@P3: straight line). This suggests that disruption of the pdx1
L204@P3: gene did not kill the hypoxic non-replicating bacillus, but
L205@P3: made it sensitive to sudden and direct oxygen exposure
L206@P3: and thereby prevented regrowth. To address the question,
L207@P3: why the ‘re-aeration phenotype’ of Dpdx1 bacilli was not
L208@P3: compensated by exogenous vitamin B6 present in all
L209@P3: culture media, we analysed the pathogen’s uptake of
L210@P3: radiolabelled pyridoxine under aerobic and hypoxic conditions (Fig. 3C). Both wild-type organisms and the Dpdx1
L211@P3: mutant did incorporate pyridoxine during the initial growth
L212@P3: phase in the anaerobic shift culture model, while oxygen
L213@P3: was present. However, no further uptake of pyridoxine
L214@P3: occurred once the two strains entered the oxygen-starved
L215@P3: non-replicating state. These results suggest that hypoxic
L216@P3: non-replicating M. tuberculosis is impaired for uptake of
L217@P3: vitamin B6.
