L239@P4: survival in vivo
L240@P4: To study the role of pdx1 in virulence, we infected immunocompetent mice with the Dpdx1 mutant and monitored
L241@P4: bacterial numbers in lungs and spleen over time. The
L242@P4: pdx1 loss-of-function strain was severely attenuated in
L243@P4: vivo: at 7 days post infection, the burden of viable Dpdx1
L244@P4: bacilli in lungs was about 1.0 log lower than that of the
L245@P4: wild-type control (Fig. 4A). On day 28 the difference was
L246@P4: more than 5.0 logs and the number of Dpdx1 organisms
L247@P4: dropped below the limit of detection. From day 42
L248@P4: onwards until the end of the experiment on day 84 neither
L249@P4: Dpdx1 bacilli nor any revertants were observed. Additionally, the Dpdx1 mutant did not cause any visible gross
L250@P4: pulmonary pathology nor was it capable of infecting the
L251@P4: spleen (Fig. 4A and B). Genetic complementation
L252@P4: restored virulence, demonstrating that the severe growth
L253@P4: defect of Dpdx1 in mice was solely due to the disruption of
L254@P4: the pdx1 gene. Our data show that bacterial de novo
L255@P4: vitamin B6 biosynthesis is essential for survival and virulence of M. tuberculosis in the mouse model.
L256@P4: Discussion
L257@P4: We have demonstrated that M. tuberculosis synthesizes
L258@P4: PLP via the ‘DXP-independent’ pathway by employing a
L259@P4: synthase composed of Pdx1 and Pdx2 in line with
L260@P4: reports from other species (Fig. 1) (Dong et al., 2004;
L261@P4: Raschle et al., 2005; Tambasco-Studart et al., 2005;
L262@P4: Gengenbacher et al., 2006; Knockel et al., 2007). Disruption of the pdx1 gene made the pathogen auxotrophic for
L263@P4: vitamin B6. Furthermore, we could fully complement the
L264@P4: phenotype in vitro by pdx1 under the control of its native
L265@P4: promoter (Fig. 2A). This suggests that the downstream
L266@P4: genes tesB2 and pdx2 in the knockout and complemented strain were expressed by the hygromycin promoter and thereby allowed formation of the PLP
L267@P4: synthase by Pdx1 and Pdx2.
L268@P4: Removal of exogenous vitamin B6 during exponential
L269@P4: growth and aerated stationary phase resulted in the death
L270@P4: of the bacillus, proving the essentiality of the PLP synthase in vitro (Fig. 2B and C). Hypoxic non-replicating
L271@P4: cultures of wild-type M. tuberculosis overexpressed the
L272@P4: PLP synthase. Surprisingly, Dpdx1 bacilli were impaired
L273@P4: for regrowth upon oxygen exposure by direct plating on
L274@P4: agar, but the loss of viability was prevented by re-aeration
L275@P4: in liquid medium before cfu determination (Fig. 3B). Previous reports provided evidence that vitamin B6 can act
L276@P4: as potent quencher of reactive oxygen species (Ehrenshaft et al., 1999; Jain and Lim, 2001; Matxain et al.,
L277@P4: 2009). Directly plated hypoxic non-replicating Dpdx1
L278@P4: bacilli probably have lost viability because of a lack of
L279@P4: protection from oxygen radicals by vitamin B6. However,
L280@P4: under aerated conditions wild-type organisms and the
L281@P4: Dpdx1 mutant showed similar susceptibility to oxidative
L282@P4: stress inducing agents (Fig. S2). An increased demand of
L283@P4: PLP during dormancy is unlikely, as the major functions of
L284@P4: vitamin B6 are related to amino acid metabolism and
L285@P4: therefore to growth (Cole et al., 1998; Sassetti et al.,
L286@P4: 2003; Percudani and Peracchi, 2009). The hypoxic
L287@P4: tubercle bacillus is impaired for uptake of vitamin B6 thus,
